The decrypt, thunderstorm, matrix, spotlights, laseretch, vhstape, fireworks,
bouncyballs, expand, and beams effects are adapted from TerminalTextEffects
by ChrisBuilds,
via omacom/ttfx. Both upstream projects are MIT licensed; their notices are in LICENSE.

Reference: omacom/ttfx commit 921bd551c308c235e01c5867f0199efe0691c271,
src/effects/{decrypt,thunderstorm,matrix,spotlights,laseretch,vhstape,fireworks,bouncyballs,expand,beams}.rs
(https://github.com/omacom/ttfx).
Original: https://github.com/ChrisBuilds/terminaltexteffects.

This is a new, hand-written 68k implementation for the monochrome SE/30.
It preserves the typing, fast cipher, slow cipher, and discovery sequence.
ASCII replaces Unicode cipher/block glyphs. Monochrome replaces gradients.
Typing uses four characters per step, fast cipher uses 24 two-tick symbols,
and slow cipher uses one to six symbols. Its PRNG is xorshift32. It does
not claim frame or RNG parity with the original effect or the ttfx engines.

banner.txt/banner.h adapt the installed Omarchy screensaver branding from
omacom/omarchy (https://github.com/omacom/omarchy), MIT licensed.
Its copyright and license are preserved in BRANDING-LICENSE.

Thunderstorm replaces color gradients with spatial dithering, uses a fixed
32-drop rain pool, one 26-cell strike, a 12-cell branch, and eight impact
sparks. The storm lasts 240 animation steps. Its paths and timings are
adapted to a fixed 81x26 monochrome canvas, without upstream frame parity.

Matrix uses 27 disjoint streams with delayed heads, random ASCII trails, and a
permuted column resolve. Spotlights uses three bouncing integer paths, a
precomputed squared-distance table, and a center convergence/expansion phase.
Laseretch processes two non-space targets per tick with a diagonal beam and
sparks; settled text survives overlay restoration. VHStape shifts a moving
three-row wave, occasionally glitches an extra row, adds character snow, then
redraws the original text. These are visual adaptations, not byte-for-byte
ports of upstream scenes, color gradients, paths, timings, or RNG behavior.

The direct renderer embeds the public-domain X.Org 6x13 bitmap font.
See demo-font.h for its original copyright notice and source attribution.

Fireworks launches 16-character shells every 12 steps, rises and bursts along
16 integer radial directions, then returns those characters to their targets.
Bouncyballs drops three characters every other step along their target columns,
using a 40-step OutBounce curve. Both use a fixed 64-slot pool and retain settled
text underneath overlapping particles. Expand uses a 180-step InOutQuart table
and per-axis coordinate maps. Beams uses six row/column scan heads with sparse
trail restoration, retaining illuminated text, followed by a diagonal wipe.
Timings, particle limits, paths, and monochrome symbols are adapted to the SE/30.
